docs/measurement.md:44:| Repeats, counted once | RDT lists one component under each file that re-exports it, and names a default export after the file or directory (antd's `col/index.tsx` default-exports `Col` from `../grid` and RDT reports `col` there; Caliper reports the defining file). An entry that Caliper does not report in its own file pairs by evidence: where the default export, or the named export, of RDT's file is defined (followed through `export default X`, `import`, `export { X } from` and `export { default as X }`), or else the file that declares its props when Caliper reports a component of that name there. The same evidence works inside one file: a default export that is another name (`const InternalButton = ...; export default InternalButton`, which RDT calls `button`), or the component whose `displayName` RDT reports. Both are guarded: the RDT name must be the file's or directory's own, so a member such as `Form.List` is never paired with `Form`, and a Caliper key that another RDT entry matches exactly is never a target. A call around the name (`memo(Foo)`, `React.forwardRef(Foo)`, `Object.assign(Foo, { Item })`), a type assertion (`Foo as T`, `Foo satisfies T`, `Foo!`) and a chain of local aliases (`const A = memo(B)`) lead to the declaration; Caliper's `displayName` for it counts as its name. Entries that pair with the same Caliper component are one reference item. |
docs/measurement.md:112:The **universe** is every own prop the checker finds on an exported function whose only parameter, or `props` parameter, is the props type, and which can be a component. React's inherited DOM attributes, `ref` and `key` are not in it. The checker has no notion of a component, so a function is left out when its shape says it is none, by the rules that clean the reference for recall (the table above): more than half of its props are declared in TypeScript's `lib.*.d.ts` (the parameter is an `Element` or a `String`), its name is a hook's, its only props are a context Provider's `value` and `children`, its name starts lowercase and is no tag's (not the file's or directory's name, no `@types/react` prop), or every prop is a `Promise`. The rules are one definition: `apps/oracle/src/component-shape.json` holds the patterns, `apps/oracle/src/component-shape.ts` applies them to the checker's functions, `scripts/weekly.py` `exclusion` to RDT's entries, and both run the cases of `apps/oracle/tests/fixtures/component-shape-cases.json`, so they cannot drift apart. The paths those rules read, and the oracle's own (where the checker declares a prop, which declarations are `@types/react`'s), are classed by one file, `apps/oracle/src/path-classes.json` (a dependency is anything under `node_modules`, `@types/react` and TypeScript's `lib.*.d.ts` are told apart, `\` is read as `/`): `apps/oracle/src/path-class.ts` and `scripts/oracle_data.py` read it, and `apps/oracle/tests/path-class.test.ts` and `scripts/test_path_classes.py` run the same cases of `apps/oracle/tests/fixtures/path-class-cases.json`. `scripts/rdt.mjs` reads it too, to leave `@types/react`'s props out of its dump. A function Caliper itself reports as a component stays in the universe whatever its shape. A default export is named from its declaration (`export default function Foo` is `Foo`; one with no name of its own takes its file's, PascalCased, as Caliper names it), which is how it meets Caliper's and RDT's entries. A component is its declaration, not an export name: `export default function Foo` followed by `export { Foo }` or `export { Foo as Bar }` is one component, counted once under the declaration's own name, and a re-export (`export { Foo } from './Foo'`, `export *`) is counted only in the file that declares it, never in the importing file; two components of one name in different files are two (`declaredExports` in `apps/oracle/src/checker-components.ts`, which both the oracle and the reference universe read). The universe still holds some functions that are not components, because the rules read shape and not intent, so read the shares as a comparison between tools, not as a share of real components. For each tool the record has, in `oracle.tools`:
docs/measurement.md:155:**How the oracle pairs a component with Caliper's (`oracle.universe_version` 3, ADR 0027).** A checker component is found once per declaration (a default export and `export { Foo }` of one function are one), and paired with the Caliper component whose canonical JSON `exports` lists that file and one of the names the module exports the declaration under. Until version 3 the pairing normalised display names (path, `.` prefix and every non-alphanumeric character stripped), so `_Card` and `Card` in one file were one name and neither paired, and `Menu.Item` could pair with an `Item` export. RDT and react-docgen name a component only by name, so they are still matched on the name they list it by, as the checker's export is named (`listedName` in `apps/oracle/src/checker-components.ts`), without the normalisation. The weekly numbers move on the first run at version 3 (shared props, agreement and RDT's and react-docgen's scores, mostly where one file has several components or a renamed export); the weekly does not compare them with a record of another universe version.

